**Why this style landed in the defaults**

Python became widely used outside formal training during the 1990s, and many of the packages built in those first years still go through very different review than newer ones. Learnings from that time shaped the coding style that teams have adopted.

**Explicit loops instead of dense comprehensions**

Code reviewers in these mature projects routinely flag dense comprehensions. A reviewer who sees a one-liner that builds a dictionary from filtered records has to mentally unpack the filtering condition, the key type, the value type, and any error handling, all in one line. A stack trace from such a line gives no information about which step failed. Teams that review heavily therefore expect an explicit `for` statement with an `if` check and a clear `continue` for the cases that should drop.
For example, compare the explicit form from earlier with its comprehension equivalent:

```python
# Preferred in most heavily reviewed Python
result = {}
for item in items:
    if item.quality > 0.8:
        result[item.id] = item.name

# Dense comprehension the same reviewers would flag
result = {item.id: item.name for item in items if item.quality > 0.8}
```

The two produce identical output. The difference is what happens when something breaks. In a review comment, a dense comprehension is usually a simple comment asking for a loop. In a debugging session, the same code split across a few explicit lines usually takes seconds to fix.

This convention is the reason the loop-and-if form is the standard in most review checklists for Python projects, and it is one reason these projects tend to use explicit loops in their reference solutions.

**Negotiating with assistants**

When you ask an LLM coding assistant to write a function such as this one, many reviewers will ask first for explicit loops rather than comprehensions. Try it in your own prompts. The rewrite is usually small, and it keeps the function readable during review.

